## 2. BRAND IDENTITY

### Official brand name

Build Buds

Always spell the name exactly as “Build Buds” in visible branding and page copy. Do not rename the platform, invent an alternative spelling or create unrelated sub-brands.

### Brand meaning

Build Buds represents a community of young builders who make things, share knowledge, exchange ideas and help one another improve.

The word “Build” represents creation, experimentation, programming, design and problem-solving.

The word “Buds” represents friendship, collaboration, belonging and the idea that no creator has to build alone.

The personality of the brand should feel:

- Playful, optimistic and energetic.
- Creative, curious and imaginative.
- Welcoming to beginners and experienced student creators.
- Community-oriented rather than corporate.
- Modern and technologically capable without looking cold or overly futuristic.
- Youthful without looking like a website designed only for very young children.
- Professional enough for schools, teachers and parents to take seriously.

The website should feel like a place students would genuinely enjoy visiting, not like another school portal full of boxes, tables, formal headings and administrative menus.

### Typography

Use **Chewy**, the font selected for the Build Buds brand, as the primary expressive display typeface.

Load Chewy through Google Fonts if available in the implementation environment. Ensure the typeface actually loads in the rendered website.

Use Chewy for:

- The Build Buds wordmark when the official image logo is not yet available.
- The main hero heading.
- Important section headings.
- Selected playful labels and short decorative text.
- Major visual moments where expressive typography adds personality.

Use a clean, highly readable sans-serif typeface such as Inter for paragraph text, navigation, buttons, forms, technical labels and interface details.

Do not use Chewy for long paragraphs. Maintain excellent readability.

Use a deliberate type scale with strong contrast between the hero headline, section headings, card titles, supporting copy and captions.

Avoid excessive capitalization, overly tight text, unnecessary font styles and oversized paragraphs.

