## 7. HERO SECTION — THE MOST IMPORTANT VISUAL MOMENT

Create an outstanding hero section that immediately explains what Build Buds is and gives visitors a reason to explore.

The hero should look memorable within the first few seconds of arrival.

Use this recommended headline:

**“Big ideas. Little builds. A world of possibilities.”**

You may adjust line breaks for visual impact, but preserve the meaning and friendly personality.

Supporting headline:

“Build Buds is where young creators share their projects, exchange ideas, solve problems and build amazing things together.”

Primary CTA:

“Join Build Buds”

Secondary CTA:

“Explore Creations”

Optional small supporting line:

“Made for curious minds and builders across schools.”

Keep the messaging authentic. Do not invent a launch date, membership count, school count, growth statistic or claim that the platform already has an active user base.

### Hero composition

Create an asymmetrical, creative composition rather than centring all content in a narrow column with generic decorative blobs.

On desktop, use a balanced text-and-visual layout.

The text side should contain the heading, concise explanation and two clear calls to action.

The visual side should show an attractive composition of digital projects: a small game preview, a website preview, a creative project thumbnail and a conversation snippet.

Present them as elements of a single creative world, using deliberate layering, subtle rotation and a controlled combination of the official brand colours.

The visual composition should suggest that different people are making different kinds of things and sharing them with one another.

Possible floating captions:

- “Made by a builder.”
- “A new idea taking shape.”
- “Built. Shared. Improved.”
- “Your next project starts here.”

These should be ordinary decorative captions or contextual labels, never AI badges.

Do not create fake browser windows full of gibberish, pretend dashboards or illegible miniature content.

Use legible, carefully designed project previews.

### Hero animation

When the page loads, animate the text and visual elements into place with a smooth, controlled entrance.

Use staggered reveals, gentle movement and subtle depth.

Project previews may float very slightly or respond to pointer movement on desktop, but movement must remain restrained.

Create a satisfying hover interaction on the CTAs.

The animation should reinforce the idea of creativity coming to life.

Do not use an excessive preloader. Do not make visitors wait for an animation before they can use the site.

On mobile, simplify the composition so it does not overflow the screen, obstruct text or push the main call to action too far below the fold.

