## 15. MOTION DESIGN AND ANIMATION SYSTEM

Animation is an important part of the Build Buds identity.

Build a consistent motion system rather than adding random animations to individual components.

The motion should feel responsive, playful, smooth and purposeful.

### Entrance animations

Use staggered reveals for important text and visuals.

Introduce sections with subtle opacity and transform transitions when they enter the viewport.

Animate project previews in a coordinated sequence.

Keep content readable and usable before, during and after animation.

### Hover interactions

Give buttons, links, project cards, navigation items and interactive previews thoughtful hover feedback.

Possible effects include:

- A small vertical lift.
- A gentle scale change.
- A border or background change using the approved palette.
- A subtle image zoom.
- A small, controlled rotation for decorative project previews.
- A clear pressed state when clicked.

Avoid large jumps, aggressive scaling and animations that move content out of position.

### Decorative motion

Use a few tasteful animated shapes around the hero and selected sections.

These may drift, bounce slightly or respond to interaction.

Use only the official brand colours.

Avoid random floating symbols, excessive particles, artificial circuit-board effects and unrelated technology decoration.

### Scroll experience

Use smooth anchor navigation and restrained scroll-triggered reveals.

The website should not hijack the browser's scrolling behaviour or lock the visitor into forced animation sequences.

Do not build an elaborate scroll experience that makes the page sluggish or prevents mobile users from navigating normally.

### Performance and accessibility

Respect the user's reduced-motion preference.

Use CSS animations and lightweight JavaScript wherever practical.

Avoid heavy animation libraries unless they provide clear value.

Do not animate every word, icon, card and background simultaneously.

Ensure focus states and navigation remain visible throughout animations.

Motion must enhance the experience rather than become the experience.

