## 21. RESPONSIVE DESIGN REQUIREMENTS

The website must be designed for desktop, laptop, tablet and mobile devices from the beginning.

Do not simply scale down a desktop design.

Use responsive layouts, flexible typography, fluid spacing, suitable image dimensions and breakpoints that match the content.

Test the design conceptually and, where possible, in the browser at widths around:

- 1440 px desktop.
- 1024 px laptop or small desktop.
- 768 px tablet.
- 390 px mobile.
- 320 px small mobile.

These are practical testing targets, not the only supported sizes.

### Mobile requirements

- No horizontal overflow.
- No clipped headings or project cards.
- No oversized desktop navigation.
- No buttons extending outside their containers.
- No text that becomes too small to read.
- No floating decoration obstructing important content.
- No fixed-height hero section that cuts off content.
- No hover-only functionality.
- No inaccessible mobile menu.
- No excessive spacing that forces visitors to scroll through unnecessary blank areas.

Stack split layouts logically.

Make button placement intuitive and touch-friendly.

Keep important calls to action easy to locate.

Use horizontal scrolling only where it genuinely improves a component, and provide clear visual cues when used.

Do not hide essential information merely to make the mobile page look shorter.

